WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.898
Technically strong response work can still fail operationally if communication is poor.

2
00:00:05.018 --> 00:00:11.549
Incidents affect systems, people, business services, legal obligations, privacy expectations, customer trust, and executive decision-making.

3
00:00:11.669 --> 00:00:17.383
A playbook should define communication and coordination rules before the team is under pressure.

4
00:00:17.503 --> 00:00:28.115
Internal coordination often includes the SOC, IT operations, endpoint teams, identity teams, cloud teams, network teams, application owners, legal, privacy, HR, communications, business owners, and executives.

5
00:00:28.235 --> 00:00:34.358
Not every incident needs every group, but the playbook should explain when to involve them.

6
00:00:34.478 --> 00:00:40.600
A suspicious login for a single user may start with the SOC and identity team.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:50.924
A possible data exposure may require privacy and legal review. A ransomware event may require IT operations, business continuity, communications, executives, and possibly external partners.

8
00:00:51.044 --> 00:00:57.575
External coordination must be handled carefully. Vendors may provide logs, product support, or managed service assistance.

9
00:00:57.695 --> 00:01:03.409
Customers may need updates if their service is affected. Insurers may have notification requirements.

10
00:01:03.529 --> 00:01:10.060
Regulators may have reporting timelines. Law enforcement or government partners may be appropriate in some circumstances.

11
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:21.200
The playbook should not make every responder responsible for external statements. It should identify who is authorized to coordinate outside the organization and when escalation is required.

12
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:29.483
Good playbook communication rules answer four questions: who updates whom, when, through which channel, and with what level of detail.

13
00:01:29.603 --> 00:01:38.175
A technical channel may be appropriate for responder coordination. A ticket or case record may be the official system of record.

14
00:01:38.295 --> 00:01:45.233
Executive updates should be concise and focused on impact, current status, decisions needed, and next update time.

15
00:01:45.353 --> 00:01:51.476
Legal or privacy discussions may require restricted channels. Sensitive evidence should not be posted broadly.

16
00:01:51.596 --> 00:01:59.759
Responders should avoid speculation. It is acceptable to say that something is under investigation, suspected, confirmed, contained, recovered, or unresolved.

17
00:01:59.879 --> 00:02:07.226
It is risky to guess about root cause, impact, attribution, or data exposure before evidence supports the statement.

18
00:02:07.346 --> 00:02:16.326
Stakeholders need clarity, not confident uncertainty. A playbook can help by providing status language and update templates that separate facts from assumptions.

19
00:02:16.446 --> 00:02:24.609
Escalation is not a sign of failure. It is how the organization brings the right authority to the right decision.

20
00:02:24.729 --> 00:02:30.035
Some containment actions may affect production services. Some notifications may have legal consequences.

21
00:02:30.155 --> 00:02:38.727
Some recovery choices may require business risk acceptance. A playbook should identify severity thresholds, escalation contacts, backup contacts, and decision owners.

22
00:02:38.847 --> 00:02:47.010
When communication is planned, responders can focus on the incident instead of inventing coordination paths in the middle of it.
